# Carrier Wound #28: A Burned Agent Run Wore A Transient Label

Wound #27 (the deterministic planner-output contract miss that wore an
`adapter_unavailable` label) shipped and deployed. We re-drove a dream-shape run
on the fixed code — `run-live-20260614T165913566Z-a8bc84dc` — and it reached
terminal `blocked` with:

```
blocker.code    = adapter_unavailable
blocker.message = "Cloudflare Sandbox lane did not emit a result marker (exit 1)"
```

…the SAME transient label, the SAME blind-re-drive invitation ("re-drove for 40
minutes"). But this is a DIFFERENT wound than #27 — a sibling, one parse site
upstream.

The lane's own heartbeat proves it:

```
… pi-invoke(1781456448) | normalize-output(1781456904) | build-transcript(1781456971)
```

pi RAN. `pi-invoke` logged at +0s, `normalize-output` at +456s, `build-transcript`
at +523s. The agent did 456 seconds of real work inside a **reachable** sandbox.
The lane then aborted under `set -e` (exit 1) somewhere after `build-transcript`
and BEFORE `emit-marker`, so it committed **no result marker and no receipt**.
`parseLaneResultMarker` found nothing usable and threw "did not emit a result
marker." The old catch had exactly one move for a no-marker throw: forge an
`adapter_unavailable` outage.

That is a lie. A genuine transport outage — sandbox unreachable, image
un-pullable, clone failing — freezes the heartbeat at an EARLY step and never
reaches `pi-invoke`. A heartbeat that names `build-transcript` is proof of the
opposite: the carrier could reach the sandbox fine; the failure is **inside the
lane, after the agent already burned its run**. Deterministic. Re-driving re-burns
456 seconds of agent time to fail identically.

> If the operation failed because of any significant problem, it is likely to fail
> again if retried immediately. Some kinds of transient failures might be overcome
> with a retry (for example, dropped packets over a wireless WAN). Within the walls
> of a data center, however, the failure is probably because of something wrong
> with the other end of a connection. […] Thus, fast retries are very likely to
> fail again. […] If you cannot complete an operation because of some timeout, it
> is better for you to return a result. It can be a failure, a success, or a note
> that you've queued the work for later execution. […] In any case, just come back
> with an answer.
>
> — Michael Nygard, *Release It!*, §5.6 Timeouts (retries & "best effort")

A burned-agent-run lane IS "something wrong with the other end of a connection,"
not a dropped packet. The honest answer is a failure result that names the lane
step — not another roll of the same dice.

## Mechanism — the no-marker throw had one classification

Two distinct planner-content failure branches funnel through the SAME catch in
`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts` (the sync `runLane` path the planner uses):

- **Branch A (Wound #27, SHIPPED):** the lane committed a parseable receipt whose
  JSON was the wrong shape → `safeParse` rejected downstream →
  `PlannerBlueprintContractError` → `planner_output_invalid`.
- **Branch B (Wound #28, THIS one):** the lane committed NOTHING — pi ran,
  `normalize-output` concluded `normalized:false` (an `set +e`-guarded outcome that
  does NOT abort), the script continued to `build-transcript`, then aborted under
  `set -e` before `emit-marker`. No marker, no receipt.
  `parseLaneResultMarker(result)` throws → caught at the executor's catch → old
  code flattened it to `adapter_unavailable`.

Branch B never had a typed signal. Every no-marker throw — transport outage OR
burned-agent-run — collapsed to the same retryable code.

## Fix — classify the no-marker failure by how far the heartbeat got

The heartbeat already exists (the executor reads `/workspace/.piwf-heartbeat` and
joins the last 20 lines). We made it load-bearing for classification.

**Pure classifier** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`, three new
exports, zero new dependencies):

- `LANE_STEP_SEQUENCE` — the ordered 15-step lane sequence as a `const` tuple.
- `lastHeartbeatStep(heartbeatTail)` — pulls the trailing step token from the
  `" | "`-joined tail; returns `null` for an empty/garbled tail (fails safe).
- `laneStepReachedAgentInvocation(step)` — `true` iff `step` is `pi-invoke` or
  later. An unknown token returns `false`, so a corrupt heartbeat fails **safe**
  toward the retryable transport classification.
- `classifyAgentLaneIncompleteFailure({cause, detail, heartbeatTail, runId,
  workItemId})` — reached `pi-invoke`+ → a typed `AgentLaneIncompleteError`;
  otherwise `null` (caller keeps its transport error).

**Application layer** (`ports.ts`, zod-free, precedent
`PlannerBlueprintContractError`): typed `AgentLaneIncompleteError` carrying
`lastStep`, `reachedAgentInvocation`, `detail`, `runId`, `workItemId`, and `cause`.
Its message names the last heartbeat step and states "the agent ran but the lane
committed no usable result (lane-internal failure, NOT a transport outage)."

**Infrastructure wiring** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts`, the one catch at
~line 436): BEFORE the bare-Error transport wrap, call the classifier on the
heartbeat tail. Non-null → throw the typed error. Null → keep
`new Error("… [step heartbeat: …]")` so a genuine outage stays retryable. Both the
no-marker throw and the error-marker abort path flow through this single catch →
ONE wiring point.

**Application catch** (`workflow-app.ts`, ~line 1459): added a branch BEFORE the
`z.ZodError` one — `error instanceof AgentLaneIncompleteError` →
`block("planner_lane_incomplete", error.message)`. `proposePlan` calls
`config.runtime.runLane(...)` with no surrounding try/catch, so the typed rejection
propagates unwrapped. The dependency arrow stays correct: infra throws the
application-layer typed error; application keys on it.

**New denial code** (`schemas.ts`): `planner_lane_incomplete`, between
`planner_output_invalid` and `drive_stall_reaped`. Because `block()` writes
`terminalBlocker.code = blockedBy.code`, the operator status endpoint's
`blocker.code` flips directly from `adapter_unavailable` to
`planner_lane_incomplete`. The status page stops lying.

## Chaos tests that would have caught it (faithful doubles)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — 5 new, including a
faithful double that SLICES the REAL `mark()` function out of
`buildPiAgentLaneCommand()`'s emitted bash, runs it with `hb_path` pointed at a
tmpdir file, replays `install-pi-agent → … → build-transcript`, reads the file
back exactly as the executor does, and feeds the joined tail to the classifier:

- live `a8bc84dc` tail (`… pi-invoke | normalize-output | build-transcript`) →
  `AgentLaneIncompleteError`, `lastStep:"build-transcript"`,
  `reachedAgentInvocation:true`, preserves `cause`, says lane-internal.
- error-marker abort at `build-receipt` → `AgentLaneIncompleteError`,
  `lastStep:"build-receipt"`.
- frozen at `clone-artifacts` (before `pi-invoke`) → `null` (stays transport).
- garbled/empty heartbeat → `null` (fails safe).
- the faithful real-`mark()` double → `AgentLaneIncompleteError` end-to-end.

`tests/integration/workflow-app.test.ts` — the planner double rejects
`proposePlan()` with a real `AgentLaneIncompleteError` (lastStep
`build-transcript`); asserts blocker + terminal blocker both
`planner_lane_incomplete`, message naming the last step and "lane committed no
usable result." Its sibling ("keeps a generic planner transport failure classified
as adapter_unavailable", rejecting with a bare `Error("connection reset")`) guards
the null branch — no over-reclassifying.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · `pnpm vitest run` = **424 passed**.

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

The root cause of *why* the lane aborted after `build-transcript` is still
UNCONFIRMED. The most likely candidate is `build-transcript` writing a large
transcript and tripping ENOSPC (the `lite` sandbox disk), but it could also be a
git failure, a hash failure, or an OOM between steps. This fix is correct under ALL
of those hypotheses: it stops the blind retry of a deterministic lane-internal
failure and surfaces the EXACT step the lane died after. The NEXT honest live run
will name the true step instead of us guessing — and only THEN do we harden that
step (e.g. an input-size fast-cap for normalize/transcript), with evidence, not on
a guess. We did NOT reclassify other `adapter_unavailable` sites — only the class
with a live receipt.

## Follow-ups (carried, not yet done)

- **Path 2** — a committed `status:failed` planner receipt currently maps to
  `adapter_unavailable` in `cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts:423`; fix when it
  surfaces with a live receipt (→ `planner_output_invalid`).
- **Async dispatch/poll lane no-result path** — the execution-lane path
  (`dispatchLane`/`pollLane`/`readLaneReceipt`) does NOT call
  `parseLaneResultMarker`; a separate potential wound, deferred (no live receipt).
- **Deterministic re-drive fast-fail** — a `planner_lane_incomplete` block should
  not re-run the planner on the next DO-alarm; fold into the pause/re-arm seam
  (shared with #27's follow-up).
- **build-transcript / normalize input-size fast-cap** — do WITH evidence after
  the next honest run names the dying step, NOT on a guess.
- Pre-existing zombie run (separate).

## The carrier discipline (eighth wound, same shape)

Error *classification* is a carrier property: the lane can do 456 seconds of real
agent work and the carrier still lies about the outcome. "Observe the run, don't
stare at a status page" only works if the status page is HONEST. A transient label
on a burned agent run is the carrier telling the operator to keep waiting — and to
keep re-burning 456 seconds of agent time — for something that will never change.
The heartbeat was telling the truth the whole time; the carrier just wasn't
reading it.
